{
  "dom_id": "step-q346662",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $C=\\{-3,-1,1,3\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{C}(-1)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{C}(x)$ of a set $C$ equals $1$ if $x\\in C$ and $0$ if $x\\notin C.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(x)=1[x\\in C].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(-1)=1[-1\\in C]=1[-1\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}].\n$$",
